From: Aleksei Sviridkin <f@lex.la>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: connect a late-arriving PHY at ifup

A PHY whose driver loads firmware at probe has no driver bound while that
module is still in an unmounted rootfs. phy_attach_direct falls back to the
generic driver, whose feature set lacks the modes this port is wired for, and
the port is dropped for the rest of the uptime:

  mt7530-mdio mdio-bus:1f lan4: validation of 2500base-x [...] failed: -EINVAL
  mt7530-mdio mdio-bus:1f lan4: error -22 setting up PHY for tree 0, switch 0, port 5

A built-in switch driver probes long before that rootfs is mounted. Wire the
same PHY to a MAC and nothing happens: the MAC driver on this SoC connects its
PHY from ndo_open rather than at probe.

Keep the port when the connect fails on a PHY that has no driver of its own,
and connect it from the open path instead, ahead of enabling the port, so that
a port in MLO_AN_PHY mode is not started without a PHY. A PHY whose driver has
bound in the meantime connects normally and the port comes up as usual. If the
connect at setup failed for a reason other than mode validation, the generic
driver may satisfy the port after all; an ifup that wins the race against the
module then connects on it, and the port stays on the generic driver.

The driverless state is sampled after the failed connect rather than before
it: the failed connect releases the generic driver again, while a PHY served
by a driver of its own keeps it bound.

The predicate covers every PHY named in the port's description that the
generic driver serves, not only the ones waiting for a module. A port whose
PHY is served by that driver and cannot satisfy the configured phy-mode is
therefore registered now instead of being dropped at setup, and each ifup on
it fails with a warning. Each failed ifup
binds the generic driver, re-reads its abilities over MDIO and releases it
again. That cycle used to run once at setup; on such a port it now runs per
attempt, and a PHY that declares a reset line sees it toggled each time.

An ifup that runs before the PHY driver is available fails the same way. On a
normal boot the module is loaded before the network is
configured; OpenWrt does that from its boot script, ahead of netifd. An ifup
has to be deliberately early to land in that window.

Deferring the switch probe instead does not converge. Every retry re-runs the
port setup and flaps the other ports. Measured on an MT7981B board, the module
arrived 5.4 s after the probe gave up in both trials: give-up at 22.2 s and
module at 27.6 s with a 20 s deadline, 47.3 s and 52.7 s with a 45 s one. The
arrival tracks the deadline, so raising the deadline moves the target with it.
The hotplug traffic each retry generates is the suspected reason, but that was
not measured.

The alternatives on the PHY-driver side do not cover the same ground. Building
that driver into the kernel is worse rather than better: it loads its firmware
with request_firmware_direct(), which does not fall back to the usermode
helper, and passes the error straight out of probe rather than -EPROBE_DEFER,
so a built-in driver fails once against the unmounted rootfs and is never
retried. A port is dropped this way whenever its PHY driver is not there yet
at switch probe. Baking the blobs in through CONFIG_EXTRA_FIRMWARE puts this
PHY's 144 KiB into every kernel built from that configuration, which is a
distribution decision rather than one a core change can make.

The driverless check races the driver arrival itself: the module can bind
between the failed connect and the check reading the driver pointer. That
failed attempt ran with the generic driver bound and says nothing about the
driver present now, so the connect is retried once, and only for a PHY that
had no driver when the attempt started. A port whose PHY was bound all along
keeps its single attempt.

Submitted upstream: https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/20260822155259.87146-1-f@lex.la/

The maintainer turned the approach down: binding the MAC to the PHY
from the open path is the wrong layer, and a phylink-hotplug
direction is under consideration instead. Kept here as an OpenWrt
carry until an upstream direction exists.

Signed-off-by: Aleksei Sviridkin <f@lex.la>
---
--- a/net/dsa/user.c
+++ b/net/dsa/user.c
@@ -396,6 +396,49 @@ void dsa_user_host_uc_uninstall(struct n
 		dsa_port_standalone_host_fdb_del(dp, dev->dev_addr, 0);
 }
 
+/* Returns the port's PHY node with a reference taken, or NULL if the port has
+ * no PHY node. Looks at the same properties as phylink_fwnode_phy_connect().
+ */
+static struct fwnode_handle *dsa_user_phy_fwnode(const struct dsa_port *dp)
+{
+	struct fwnode_handle *phy_fwnode;
+
+	phy_fwnode = fwnode_get_phy_node(of_fwnode_handle(dp->dn));
+
+	return IS_ERR(phy_fwnode) ? NULL : phy_fwnode;
+}
+
+/* Connect a PHY that dsa_user_phy_setup() left behind because it had no
+ * driver of its own back then. Runs before the port is enabled, so that a
+ * port in MLO_AN_PHY mode is not started without its PHY.
+ */
+static int dsa_user_late_phy_connect(struct net_device *dev)
+{
+	struct dsa_port *dp = dsa_user_to_port(dev);
+	struct fwnode_handle *phy_fwnode;
+	struct dsa_switch *ds = dp->ds;
+	u32 phy_flags = 0;
+	int err;
+
+	if (dev->phydev)
+		return 0;
+
+	phy_fwnode = dsa_user_phy_fwnode(dp);
+	if (!phy_fwnode)
+		return 0;
+
+	fwnode_handle_put(phy_fwnode);
+
+	if (ds->ops->get_phy_flags)
+		phy_flags = ds->ops->get_phy_flags(ds, dp->index);
+
+	err = phylink_of_phy_connect(dp->pl, dp->dn, phy_flags);
+	if (err)
+		netdev_warn(dev, "could not connect PHY: %pe\n", ERR_PTR(err));
+
+	return err;
+}
+
 static int dsa_user_open(struct net_device *dev)
 {
 	struct net_device *conduit = dsa_user_to_conduit(dev);
@@ -412,6 +455,10 @@ static int dsa_user_open(struct net_devi
 	if (err)
 		goto out;
 
+	err = dsa_user_late_phy_connect(dev);
+	if (err)
+		goto out_del_host_uc;
+
 	err = dsa_port_enable_rt(dp, dev->phydev);
 	if (err)
 		goto out_del_host_uc;
@@ -2653,11 +2700,37 @@ static int dsa_user_phy_connect(struct n
 	return phylink_connect_phy(dp->pl, user_dev->phydev);
 }
 
+/* Whether the port's PHY is served by the generic driver rather than by one
+ * of its own. Only meaningful after a failed connect, which releases the
+ * generic driver again.
+ */
+static bool dsa_user_phy_lacks_driver(const struct dsa_port *dp)
+{
+	struct fwnode_handle *phy_fwnode;
+	struct phy_device *phydev;
+	bool lacks_driver;
+
+	phy_fwnode = dsa_user_phy_fwnode(dp);
+	if (!phy_fwnode)
+		return false;
+
+	phydev = fwnode_phy_find_device(phy_fwnode);
+	fwnode_handle_put(phy_fwnode);
+	if (!phydev)
+		return false;
+
+	lacks_driver = !READ_ONCE(phydev->mdio.dev.driver);
+	put_device(&phydev->mdio.dev);
+
+	return lacks_driver;
+}
+
 static int dsa_user_phy_setup(struct net_device *user_dev)
 {
 	struct dsa_port *dp = dsa_user_to_port(user_dev);
 	struct device_node *port_dn = dp->dn;
 	struct dsa_switch *ds = dp->ds;
+	bool had_driver, retried = false;
 	u32 phy_flags = 0;
 	int ret;
 
@@ -2680,6 +2753,8 @@ static int dsa_user_phy_setup(struct net
 	if (ds->ops->get_phy_flags)
 		phy_flags = ds->ops->get_phy_flags(ds, dp->index);
 
+	had_driver = !dsa_user_phy_lacks_driver(dp);
+connect:
 	ret = phylink_of_phy_connect(dp->pl, port_dn, phy_flags);
 	if (ret == -ENODEV && ds->user_mii_bus) {
 		/* We could not connect to a designated PHY or SFP, so try to
@@ -2688,6 +2763,28 @@ static int dsa_user_phy_setup(struct net
 		ret = dsa_user_phy_connect(user_dev, dp->index, phy_flags);
 	}
 	if (ret) {
+		if (dsa_user_phy_lacks_driver(dp)) {
+			/* Not known to be reachable from the internal MDIO bus
+			 * fallback, which assigns user_dev->phydev before it
+			 * connects, but do not hand the open path a leftover.
+			 */
+			user_dev->phydev = NULL;
+
+			netdev_info(user_dev,
+				    "PHY has no driver, connecting it at open\n");
+			return 0;
+		}
+
+		/* A driver that was not there before this attempt is one that
+		 * bound while it ran: the failure came from the generic driver
+		 * and says nothing about this one, so try once more. A port
+		 * that had its driver all along keeps the single attempt.
+		 */
+		if (!had_driver && !retried) {
+			retried = true;
+			goto connect;
+		}
+
 		netdev_err(user_dev, "failed to connect to PHY: %pe\n",
 			   ERR_PTR(ret));
 		dsa_port_phylink_destroy(dp);
